CAT = "cadcCounsel"; SEG = "CNS"
Q = [
("Which phrase is person-centered language?",
 "A person with an SUD", ["An addict", "A junkie", "A chronic substance abuser"],
 "Person-centered language puts the person before the condition and avoids labels that carry stigma and can discourage people from seeking help."),
("A client says she remembers best by doing things. The counselor should favor:",
 "Role-play and practice", ["Long reading lists", "Lectures only", "Written handouts to read alone"],
 "Matching methods to a client's learning style helps skills stick. Hands-on learners do better rehearsing refusal and coping skills than reading about them."),
("A counselor faces the client, leans slightly forward and keeps an open posture. This nonverbal skill is called:",
 "Attending", ["Confronting", "Probing", "Interpreting"],
 "Attending behavior uses posture, eye contact and tone to show the client the counselor is fully present and listening."),
("Which counselor behavior most builds rapport in a first session?",
 "Accurate empathy", ["Early confrontation", "Giving quick advice", "Sharing her own story"],
 "TIP 35 identifies counselor empathy as central to engagement. Confronting or advising too early tends to raise discord and push clients away."),
("TIP 35 reports that an argumentative, confrontational counseling style tends to:",
 "Increase discord", ["Speed recovery", "Lower dropout rates", "Build trust"],
 "SAMHSA's TIP 35 notes that confrontational approaches tend to increase client resistance and discord rather than motivate change."),
("In TIP 35, a summary that gathers all of a client's change talk from many conversations, useful when making a change plan, is a:",
 "Recapitulation summary", ["Collecting summary", "Linking summary", "Transitional summary"],
 "TIP 35 describes the recapitulation summary as gathering all the change talk of many conversations. A linking summary ties one statement to an earlier one, and a transitional summary wraps up a task."),
("\"When you say you're done, do you mean done with drinking or done with treatment?\" This response is:",
 "Clarification", ["Reflection", "Reframing", "Interpretation"],
 "Clarification asks the client to make an unclear statement more specific so the counselor does not act on a wrong assumption."),
("Which kind of feedback is most useful to a client?",
 "Specific and descriptive", ["Vague and general", "Delayed for several weeks", "Focused on character flaws"],
 "Feedback works best when it describes specific behavior soon after it happens, rather than judging the person."),
("A client wants to quit but fears losing her friends who use. TIP 35 describes this mixed feeling as:",
 "Normal ambivalence", ["Denial to confront", "A personality disorder", "No motivation at all"],
 "TIP 35 states that ambivalence about change is normal. The counselor helps the client explore and resolve it rather than treating it as denial."),
("Asking a client to list the pros and cons of using and of changing is a:",
 "Decisional balance", ["Genogram", "Contingency contract", "Discharge plan"],
 "A decisional balance helps the client weigh the costs and benefits of change; TIP 35 includes decisional balance scales for alcohol and drugs."),
("A client has set a quit date next week and asked where meetings are held. Which stage of change fits?",
 "Preparation", ["Contemplation", "Action", "Maintenance"],
 "In preparation the person intends to act soon and is making a plan. Action begins when the behavior change itself starts."),
("TIP 35 describes which kind of summary as gathering both sustain talk and change talk from one session, while highlighting the change talk?",
 "Ambivalence summary", ["Recapitulation summary", "Linking summary", "Collecting summary"],
 "An ambivalence summary acknowledges sustain talk but reinforces change talk from the session. A recapitulation summary gathers change talk across many conversations."),
("Eight months after quitting, a client is working on staying stopped. Which stage of change fits?",
 "Maintenance", ["Action", "Preparation", "Contemplation"],
 "In maintenance the person has made the change and works to sustain it and prevent a return to use."),
("For a client not yet considering change, TIP 35 says the counselor should mainly:",
 "Raise doubt and concern", ["Set a quit date", "Teach refusal skills", "Plan for aftercare housing"],
 "TIP 35 lists raising doubts and concerns about substance use as the key strategy for precontemplation. Planning and skills fit later stages."),
("A client's belief that she can actually make a change is called:",
 "Self-efficacy", ["Self-disclosure", "Codependency", "Countertransference"],
 "Self-efficacy is confidence in one's ability to change. Supporting it is a core part of motivational counseling and appears in the FRAMES approach."),
("In motivational interviewing, the counselor's urge to fix the problem and tell the client what to do is called the:",
 "Righting reflex", ["Halo effect", "Placebo effect", "Pygmalion effect"],
 "TIP 35 uses Miller and Rollnick's term righting reflex and advises counselors to resist it, because pushing change tends to make clients argue against it."),
("Before giving information, the counselor asks what the client already knows, then shares, then asks what she thinks. This is:",
 "Elicit-Provide-Elicit", ["Ask-Tell-Close", "Teach-Back First", "Advise-Confront-Review"],
 "TIP 35 recommends Elicit-Provide-Elicit for exchanging information in a way that respects the client's knowledge and keeps her involved."),
("\"Part of you wants to stop, and part of you will miss the way it relaxes you.\" This response is a:",
 "Double-sided reflection", ["Closed question", "Direct confrontation", "Leading question"],
 "A double-sided reflection captures both sides of the client's ambivalence in one statement, which TIP 35 recommends when clients voice mixed feelings."),
("A client snaps, \"You just don't get it.\" TIP 35 treats this mainly as:",
 "Discord in the relationship", ["Proof of denial", "Grounds for discharge", "A symptom to diagnose"],
 "TIP 35 describes this tension as discord, a signal about the counseling relationship that the counselor responds to, not a client trait to confront."),
("\"You kept your appointment even after a hard week.\" This response is an:",
 "Affirmation", ["Interpretation", "Confrontation", "Open question"],
 "An affirmation recognizes a client's genuine effort or strength, which builds confidence and rapport."),
]
